# 1. Variables & Data Types

JavaScript variables store values that can be used and manipulated throughout your program.

The three primary variable declarations are:

```text
var
let
const
```

---

## `var`

`var` is the older way of declaring variables.

```javascript
var name = "Alex";
```

Characteristics:

```text
Function-scoped
Can be reassigned
Can be redeclared
Hoisted
```

Modern JavaScript generally avoids `var` unless there is a specific reason to use it.

---

## `let`

Use `let` when a variable's value needs to change.

```javascript
let score = 10;

score = 20;
```

Characteristics:

```text
Block-scoped
Can be reassigned
Cannot be redeclared in the same scope
```

Example:

```javascript
let age = 21;

age = 22;
```

---

## `const`

Use `const` when the variable should not be reassigned.

```javascript
const user = "Dev";
```

Characteristics:

```text
Block-scoped
Cannot be reassigned
Cannot be redeclared in the same scope
```

Prefer `const` by default.

Use `let` when reassignment is required.

---

## Important: `const` Objects and Arrays Can Still Change

`const` prevents reassignment of the variable itself.

It does **not** make an object or array immutable.

```javascript
const user = {
    name: "Alex"
};

user.name = "Sam";
```

This works.

But this does not:

```javascript
const user = {
    name: "Alex"
};

user = {
    name: "Sam"
};
```

The same concept applies to arrays.

```javascript
const fruits = ["apple"];

fruits.push("orange");
```

This is allowed.

---

## Common JavaScript Data Types

JavaScript commonly works with:

```text
String
Number
Boolean
Object
Array
Null
Undefined
BigInt
Symbol
```

---

## String

Text values are strings.

```javascript
const name = "Alex";
```

```javascript
typeof name;
// "string"
```

---

## Number

JavaScript uses the `number` type for both integers and floating-point numbers.

```javascript
const age = 21;
const price = 49.99;
```

```javascript
typeof age;
// "number"
```

---

## Boolean

Boolean values represent true or false.

```javascript
const isDev = true;
const isAdmin = false;
```

---

## Array

Arrays store multiple values.

```javascript
const skills = [
    "JavaScript",
    "CSS",
    "HTML"
];
```

---

## Object

Objects store related data as key-value pairs.

```javascript
const user = {
    role: "dev",
    age: 21
};
```

---

## `null`

Represents an intentionally empty value.

```javascript
const result = null;
```

---

## `undefined`

Usually means a value has not yet been assigned.

```javascript
let result;

console.log(result);
// undefined
```

---

## `typeof`

Use `typeof` to inspect the type of a value.

```javascript
typeof "hello";
// "string"

typeof 42;
// "number"

typeof true;
// "boolean"
```

---

## Variable Rule of Thumb

```text
Use const first.

Use let when the value must change.

Avoid var in modern JavaScript unless you specifically need its behavior.
```

