# 2. Operators & Conditions

Operators allow JavaScript to compare values, perform calculations, and control which code runs.

---

## Comparison Operators

```javascript
const age = 21;

age >= 18;
```

Common comparison operators:

| Operator | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `===` | Strict equality |
| `!==` | Strict inequality |
| `>` | Greater than |
| `<` | Less than |
| `>=` | Greater than or equal |
| `<=` | Less than or equal |

---

## Strict Equality

Prefer:

```javascript
value === otherValue;
```

instead of:

```javascript
value == otherValue;
```

`===` compares both:

```text
Value
Type
```

Example:

```javascript
5 === 5;
// true

5 === "5";
// false
```

With loose equality:

```javascript
5 == "5";
// true
```

### Modern Rule

```text
Prefer === instead of ==
Prefer !== instead of !=
```

---

## Logical Operators

Logical operators combine multiple conditions.

| Operator | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `&&` | AND |
| `||` | OR |
| `!` | NOT |

---

## AND — `&&`

Both conditions must be true.

```javascript
const isLoggedIn = true;
const isAdmin = true;

if (isLoggedIn && isAdmin) {
    console.log("Admin access");
}
```

---

## OR — `||`

At least one condition must be true.

```javascript
const isStudent = false;
const isMember = true;

if (isStudent || isMember) {
    console.log("Discount available");
}
```

---

## NOT — `!`

Reverses a boolean value.

```javascript
const isLoggedIn = false;

if (!isLoggedIn) {
    console.log("Please log in");
}
```

---

## `if / else`

Use conditional statements to run different code depending on whether a condition is true or false.

```javascript
const age = 18;

if (age >= 18) {
    console.log("Adult");
} else {
    console.log("Minor");
}
```

---

## Multiple Conditions

```javascript
const age = 21;
const hasID = true;

if (age >= 18 && hasID) {
    console.log("Access allowed");
} else {
    console.log("Access denied");
}
```

---

## `else if`

Use `else if` when more than two outcomes are possible.

```javascript
const score = 85;

if (score >= 90) {
    console.log("A");
} else if (score >= 80) {
    console.log("B");
} else if (score >= 70) {
    console.log("C");
} else {
    console.log("Needs improvement");
}
```

---

## Ternary Operator

The ternary operator provides short conditional syntax.

```javascript
const status = age >= 18
    ? "Adult"
    : "Minor";
```

General syntax:

```javascript
condition
    ? valueIfTrue
    : valueIfFalse;
```

Best used for simple decisions.

Avoid deeply nested ternary expressions when normal `if` statements would be easier to read.

