# 5. Objects & Destructuring

Objects store related information using **key-value pairs**.

---

## Create an Object

```javascript
const user = {
    name: "Alex",
    age: 21
};
```

---

## Access Object Values

### Dot Notation

```javascript
user.name;
```

### Bracket Notation

```javascript
user["age"];
```

Dot notation is generally preferred when the property name is known and valid as an identifier.

Bracket notation is useful when the property name is dynamic.

```javascript
const property = "name";

console.log(user[property]);
```

---

## Update an Object

Change an existing property:

```javascript
user.age = 22;
```

Add a new property:

```javascript
user.role = "developer";
```

---

## Destructuring

Destructuring extracts object properties into variables.

Instead of:

```javascript
const name = user.name;
const age = user.age;
```

Use:

```javascript
const {
    name,
    age
} = user;
```

---

## Example

```javascript
const product = {
    title: "Laptop",
    price: 49999,
    inStock: true
};

const {
    title,
    price
} = product;

console.log(title, price);
```

---

## Rename While Destructuring

```javascript
const user = {
    name: "Alex"
};

const {
    name: userName
} = user;

console.log(userName);
```

---

## Default Values

```javascript
const user = {
    name: "Alex"
};

const {
    name,
    role = "user"
} = user;
```

---

## Nested Destructuring

```javascript
const user = {
    name: "Alex",
    address: {
        city: "Chicago"
    }
};

const {
    address: {
        city
    }
} = user;
```

---

## Object Rule of Thumb

Use destructuring when it makes access to object values cleaner and easier to understand.

