# 7. DOM Manipulation

The **DOM (Document Object Model)** allows JavaScript to interact with the visible webpage.

JavaScript can:

```text
Select elements
Change text
Change HTML
Change styles
Add/remove classes
Create elements
Remove elements
Respond to events
```

---

## Select an Element

Use `document.querySelector()` to select the first element matching a CSS selector.

```javascript
const title = document.querySelector(
    ".title"
);
```

Select by ID:

```javascript
const button = document.querySelector(
    "#btn"
);
```

---

## Select Multiple Elements

Use:

```javascript
const items = document.querySelectorAll(
    ".item"
);
```

---

## Change Text

```javascript
title.textContent = "Hello JavaScript";
```

---

## Change HTML

```javascript
title.innerHTML = "<strong>Hello</strong>";
```

Use `innerHTML` carefully when content can come from users or other untrusted sources.

For plain text, prefer:

```javascript
textContent
```

---

## Change Styles

Styles can be changed directly:

```javascript
title.style.color = "blue";
```

Another example:

```javascript
title.style.fontSize = "2rem";
```

For substantial styling changes, CSS classes are usually preferable.

---

## Add a Class

```javascript
title.classList.add("active");
```

---

## Remove a Class

```javascript
title.classList.remove("active");
```

---

## Toggle a Class

```javascript
title.classList.toggle("dark");
```

---

## Check for a Class

```javascript
title.classList.contains("active");
```

---

## Event + DOM Example

```javascript
const button = document.querySelector(
    "#btn"
);

const box = document.querySelector(
    ".box"
);

button.addEventListener("click", () => {
    box.classList.toggle("active");
});
```

This connects JavaScript behavior to visible webpage elements.

