# 8. Events & Forms

Events allow JavaScript to react to user actions such as:

```text
Clicks
Typing
Form submissions
Mouse movement
Keyboard input
Focus changes
```

---

## Click Event

```javascript
button.addEventListener(
    "click",
    () => {
        console.log("Clicked");
    }
);
```

---

## Input Event

The `input` event runs whenever the value changes.

```javascript
input.addEventListener(
    "input",
    (event) => {
        console.log(event.target.value);
    }
);
```

---

## Event Object

The callback can receive information about the event.

```javascript
button.addEventListener(
    "click",
    (event) => {
        console.log(event);
    }
);
```

A common property is:

```javascript
event.target
```

which references the element that triggered the event.

---

## Form Submission

```javascript
form.addEventListener(
    "submit",
    (event) => {
        event.preventDefault();

        console.log("Form submitted");
    }
);
```

---

## `preventDefault()`

Browsers have default behaviors for many events.

For example, submitting a normal HTML form can cause navigation or a page reload.

```javascript
event.preventDefault();
```

prevents that default action.

---

## Read a Form Value

```javascript
const name = nameInput.value;

console.log(name);
```

---

## Complete Form Example

```javascript
const form = document.querySelector(
    "#form"
);

const email = document.querySelector(
    "#email"
);

form.addEventListener(
    "submit",
    (event) => {
        event.preventDefault();

        console.log(email.value);
    }
);
```

---

## Important Form Rule

Do **not** automatically use `preventDefault()` on every form submission.

Use it when JavaScript is intentionally handling the submission itself.

For example:

```javascript
form.addEventListener(
    "submit",
    async (event) => {
        event.preventDefault();

        // Validate and submit with JavaScript.
    }
);
```

