# 9. Async JavaScript & Fetch API

Asynchronous JavaScript allows the program to wait for operations such as:

```text
API requests
Network responses
Database operations
Timers
File operations
```

without freezing the rest of the application.

---

## Promise

A Promise represents a value that may become available later.

Example:

```javascript
fetch(url)
    .then((response) => response.json())
    .then((data) => {
        console.log(data);
    });
```

A Promise can generally be:

```text
Pending
Fulfilled
Rejected
```

---

## `async`

Declare an asynchronous function using `async`.

```javascript
async function getData() {
    // Async code
}
```

An `async` function always returns a Promise.

---

## `await`

`await` pauses execution **inside the async function** until the Promise settles.

```javascript
async function getData() {
    const response = await fetch(url);
    const data = await response.json();

    console.log(data);
}
```

---

## `try / catch`

Use `try / catch` to handle errors.

```javascript
try {
    const response = await fetch(url);
} catch (error) {
    console.error(error);
}
```

---

## Fetch API Example

```javascript
async function getUsers() {
    try {
        const response = await fetch(
            "https://api.example.com/users"
        );

        const data = await response.json();

        console.log(data);
    } catch (error) {
        console.error(
            "Failed to fetch data",
            error
        );
    }
}
```

---

## Better Fetch Error Handling

A `fetch()` request does not automatically reject merely because the server returns an HTTP error such as `404` or `500`.

Check `response.ok`:

```javascript
async function getUsers() {
    try {
        const response = await fetch(
            "https://api.example.com/users"
        );

        if (!response.ok) {
            throw new Error(
                `HTTP error: ${response.status}`
            );
        }

        const data = await response.json();

        console.log(data);
    } catch (error) {
        console.error(
            "Failed to fetch data:",
            error
        );
    }
}
```

---

## POST Request

Send data to an API:

```javascript
async function createUser(user) {
    const response = await fetch(
        "https://api.example.com/users",
        {
            method: "POST",
            headers: {
                "Content-Type": "application/json"
            },
            body: JSON.stringify(user)
        }
    );

    if (!response.ok) {
        throw new Error(
            `HTTP error: ${response.status}`
        );
    }

    return response.json();
}
```

Usage:

```javascript
const user = await createUser({
    name: "Alex",
    role: "developer"
});
```

---

## Promise Syntax vs `async/await`

Promise chaining:

```javascript
fetch(url)
    .then((response) => response.json())
    .then((data) => {
        console.log(data);
    })
    .catch((error) => {
        console.error(error);
    });
```

Equivalent `async/await` style:

```javascript
async function getData() {
    try {
        const response = await fetch(url);
        const data = await response.json();

        console.log(data);
    } catch (error) {
        console.error(error);
    }
}
```

For many workflows, `async/await` is easier to read.

